## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a spacing constraint and path reconstruction, a common interview variation.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix with constrained subsequences
- 2D state and reconstruction pointers
- Handling infeasible states and space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// state definitions: dp[i][t], par[i][t]
function solve(A, n, k, d) {
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) dp[i][1] = A[i-1];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      // skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i
      if (i - d >= 1 && dp[i-d][t-1] !== NEG) {
        const val = dp[i-d][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = i-d; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the indices from par to return the actual solution?
- How can you optimize space without changing asymptotic time?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests understanding of dynamic programming with non-overlapping segments where the segment value has a quadratic structure. It challenges candidates to handle state transitions, reconstruction, and complexity analysis systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix arrays with block count tracking
- Transition by considering all possible block endpoints
- Prefix sum optimization for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Backpointers for optimal solution reconstruction
- Time-space trade-off analysis

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function maxSubarrayValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + A[i];
  }

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prevStart = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prevStart[i][t] = prevStart[i-1][t];
      }

      // End block at i
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const blockValue = (prefix[i] - prefix[j-1]) * (i-j+1);
        const candidate = dp[j-1][t-1] + blockValue;
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prevStart[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const result = [];
  let currI = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && currI > 0) {
    const start = prevStart[currI][currT];
    if (start !== -1 && start !== prevStart[currI-1][currT]) {
      result.unshift([start-1, currI-1]);
      currI = start - 1;
      currT--;
    } else {
      currI--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], subarrays: result };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) if all array elements are non-negative?
- What techniques could reduce space complexity to O(n) while maintaining reconstruction?
- How does the solution change if subarrays can overlap?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP skills with an additional dimension for tracking turns while handling obstacles and grid boundaries. It verifies understanding of initialization logic, edge cases, and how to aggregate the final answer by summing across valid turn counts.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP over cells, turn count, and last direction
- Turn counting via direction changes during transitions
- Correct handling of obstacles and grid boundaries
- Time/space trade-offs and proper base case logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, K) {
  const m = grid.length;
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[m-1][n-1] === 1) return 0;

  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [0, 0])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = 1; // right
  dp[0][0][0][1] = 1; // down

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        // from left (coming from right)
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t][0]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t-1][1]; // turn
        }
        // from top (coming from down)
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t][1]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t-1][0]; // turn
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    ans += dp[m-1][n-1][t][0] + dp[m-1][n-1][t][1];
  }
  return ans;
}
```

This runs in O(m × n × K) time and O(m × n × K) space.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping contiguous subarrays with a per-subarray cost. It forces candidates to choose between extending a current subarray and starting a new one, while also testing reconstruction and space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **DP state separation**: Maintaining 'best' (completed subarrays) vs 'active' (subarray currently being built)
- **Non-overlapping interval selection** with a fixed count constraint
- **Reconstruction** of the chosen subarrays from DP tables
- **Space optimization** using rolling arrays (2D → 1D)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const active = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));

  best[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], active[i-1][t]);

      if (t > 0) {
        active[i][t] = Math.max(
          active[i-1][t] + A[i-1],
          best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c
        );
      }
    }
  }

  return best[n][k];
}
```

**Time Complexity**: O(n × k)
**Space Complexity**: O(n × k), or O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with non-uniform skip constraints and reconstruction, common in scheduling and resource-constrained selection. It ensures you can encode constraints as a forward DP and extract the chosen indices.

## Key Concepts

- DP with forward state (i, r)
- Transition skipping vs taking with jump i+gap[i]
- Path reconstruction and complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumWithGaps(A, gap, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 2 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  const go = (i, r) => {
    if (r === 0) return 0;
    if (i > n) return NEG;
    if (memo[i][r] != null) return memo[i][r];
    const skip = go(i + 1, r);
    const take = A[i - 1] + go(Math.min(n + 1, i + gap[i - 1]), r - 1);
    const best = Math.max(skip, take);
    memo[i][r] = best;
    return best;
  };

  const best = go(1, k);
  return { best, memo };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for variable k_max instead of exact k? 
- How would you reconstruct the indices efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model interval scheduling with a cap on concurrent starts, a common real-world constraint in job scheduling and ad campaigns.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a limit on number of intervals
- Preprocessing next[i] via binary search
- DP with reconstruction pointers for actual selection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch for dp and reconstruction
function maxProfitTasks(s, d, p, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const idx = [...Array(n).keys()].sort((a,b)=> s[a]-s[b]);
  // reorder s,d,p by idx as needed; compute next[] with binary search
  const next = Array(n).fill(n);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
    let limit = s[i] + d[i] - 1;
    // binary search for first j with s[j] > limit
    let lo=i+1, hi=n; while (lo<hi){
      let mid=(lo+hi)>>1; if (s[mid] > limit) hi=mid; else lo=mid+1; }
    next[i] = lo;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=n-1;i>=0;i--) {
    for (let t=1;t<=k;t++) {
      if (dp[i+1][t] >= p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i+1][t];
        take[i][t] = false;
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {profit: dp[0][k], take, next};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a DP for a non-standard segment value (length times segment max) with exact-k partitioning and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- 2D DP: dp[i][t] stores best value for first i elements using t segments
- Transition: dp[i][t] = max over j of dp[j][t-1] + (i-j) * max(A[j+1..i])
- On-the-fly max: as j decreases, update max(A[j+1..i]) incrementally
- Reconstruction: parent[i][t] stores last cut; backtrack to produce segments
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(A, n, k){
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      let maxv = -Infinity;
      for(let j=i-1;j>=t-1;j--){
        maxv = Math.max(maxv, A[j]); // adjust if 0-based/1-based indexing
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (i-j) * maxv;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let segments = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

